 "caption": "Where do you think Stonehenge's smaller stones came from? 🪨\n\nThe giant sarsens came from about 25 km away, but the smaller bluestones match no local rock. Geologists traced them to the Preseli Hills in west Wales, over 200 km (125 miles) from Stonehenge, and digs there found the rocky outcrops where Stone Age people prised pillars loose more than 5,000 years ago. Then a 2024 study in Nature traced the Altar Stone to northeast Scotland, about 750 km (460 miles) away. How the stones were moved is still debated.\n\nSend this to the friend who thinks they know Stonehenge.\n\nImages: Wikimedia Commons, Library of Congress, Yale Center for British Art, Getty Open Content via Rawpixel, Flickr (public domain / CC0; CC BY: TobyEditor (CC-BY-4.0), Arian Zwegers from Brussels, Belgium (CC-BY-2.0), Stonehenge Stone Circle News www.Stonehenge.News (CC-BY-2.0), Kieran Roy Powell (CC-BY-4.0), -JvL- (CC-BY-2.0), TobyEditor (CC-BY-4.0), TobyEditor (CC-BY-4.0), garethwiscombe (CC-BY-2.0)), Arian Zwegers from Brussels, Belgium (CC-BY-2.0), Stonehenge Stone Circle News www.Stonehenge.News (CC-BY-2.0), Kieran Roy Powell (CC-BY-4.0)). Sources: English Heritage, UCL, Nature (2024).",
